  "explanation": "Smart pointers in C++ are template classes that manage dynamic memory automatically, ensuring proper resource deallocation and preventing memory leaks. They are used to implement RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) idioms, providing safer and more expressive memory management compared to raw pointers. Modern C++ emphasizes the use of smart pointers to handle ownership semantics explicitly, with std::unique_ptr for exclusive ownership and std::shared_ptr for shared ownership scenarios.\n\nSmart pointers in C++ are template classes that manage dynamic memory automatically, ensuring proper resource deallocation and preventing memory leaks. They are used to implement RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) idioms, providing safer and more expressive memory management compared to raw pointers. Modern C++ emphasizes the use of smart pointers to handle resource management efficiently and exception-safely, reducing the risk of dangling pointers and leaks.",
